WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.072
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:09.420
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.774
Unit seven: Political Philosophy. Today you will trace authority using derives from / stems from.

4
00:00:16.074 --> 00:00:18.854
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.975
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.817 --> 00:00:25.224
Social contract.

7
00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:30.527
Social contract means an imagined agreement that grounds government in consent.

8
00:00:31.117 --> 00:00:34.579
Social contract theory justifies the state by agreement.

9
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:38.184
Your turn. Say: Social contract.

10
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:42.592
Social contract.

11
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.456
Sovereignty.

12
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.344
Sovereignty means supreme authority within a territory.

13
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.867
Sovereignty belongs to the people in a democracy.

14
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:55.159
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty.

15
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.326
Sovereignty.

16
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:01.131
Justice.

17
00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:04.662
Justice means fair and morally right treatment.

18
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.062
Justice requires that like cases be treated alike.

19
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:11.442
Your turn. Say: Justice.

20
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.452
Justice.

21
00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:17.380
Legitimacy.

22
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:21.405
Legitimacy means the widely accepted right to rule.

23
00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:24.899
Legitimacy fades when elections are rigged.

24
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:27.869
Your turn. Say: Legitimacy.

25
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:31.955
Legitimacy.

26
00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:34.171
Natural rights.

27
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:38.971
Natural rights means rights held by all people regardless of law.

28
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:42.639
Locke argued that natural rights include life and liberty.

29
00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.926
Your turn. Say: Natural rights.

30
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:50.116
Natural rights.

31
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.072
State of nature.

32
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:57.103
State of nature means a hypothetical condition before government exists.

33
00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:59.623
Hobbes feared the state of nature.

34
00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:02.776
Your turn. Say: State of nature.

35
00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:07.134
State of nature.

36
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:08.768
Consent.

37
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:11.957
Consent means voluntary agreement.

38
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.165
Consent is what separates authority from force.

39
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.984
Your turn. Say: Consent.

40
00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:21.834
Consent.

41
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:24.014
Political obligation.

42
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:28.630
Political obligation means the duty citizens have to obey the law.

43
00:02:29.217 --> 00:02:32.667
Political obligation is debated when laws seem unjust.

44
00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:36.214
Your turn. Say: Political obligation.

45
00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:40.544
Political obligation.

46
00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:45.031
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:49.497
Visal, why should I obey a government I never chose?

48
00:02:50.583 --> 00:02:58.237
Theorists say legitimacy derives from the consent of the governed, even when that consent is tacit.

49
00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.593
And if a government ignores its people?

50
00:03:02.683 --> 00:03:10.444
Its authority stems from consent, so abuse can erode it. Locke thought natural rights limited what any state could demand.

51
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.817
So obligation is conditional?

52
00:03:14.917 --> 00:03:22.759
For many philosophers, yes. Political obligation derives from fair treatment, not from power alone.

53
00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:25.470
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:30.093
Grammar focus: Source-of language (derives from, stems from).

55
00:03:30.393 --> 00:03:31.510
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.217 --> 00:03:34.901
Legitimacy derives from the consent of the governed.

57
00:03:35.817 --> 00:03:38.524
Authority stems from fair treatment, not power.

58
00:03:39.417 --> 00:03:42.496
Political obligation flows from reciprocal duties.

59
00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:51.415
Name the source with derive from, stem from, flow from, spring from. All take 'from' (never 'of' or 'by'): legitimacy derives from consent.

60
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.859
Be careful! Don't say: Legitimacy derives of consent.

61
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.717
Say: Legitimacy derives from consent.

62
00:04:01.017 --> 00:04:02.238
derive from (not 'of').

63
00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:05.825
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:06.633 --> 00:04:07.762
Why Obey?.

65
00:04:08.062 --> 00:04:12.477
Political philosophy asks what justifies the power that governments exercise.

66
00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:18.589
Hobbes argued that without a sovereign, life in the state of nature would be a war of all against all.

67
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:26.824
Locke replied that legitimacy derives from the consent of the governed, and that rulers who violate natural rights forfeit their authority.

68
00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:34.953
Modern theorists add that political obligation stems from fairness: those who receive the benefits of cooperation owe their share.

69
00:04:35.967 --> 00:04:40.335
Whatever the account, authority that rests on force alone is rarely stable.

70
00:04:41.433 --> 00:04:45.261
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:46.117 --> 00:04:49.080
Legitimacy derives blank the consent of the governed.

72
00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.255
A, from. B, of. C, to.

73
00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.486
The answer is A: from.

74
00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.276
Supreme authority within a territory is blank.

75
00:05:01.576 --> 00:05:05.509
A, justice. B, sovereignty. C, consent.

76
00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:11.206
The answer is B: sovereignty.

77
00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.464
A hypothetical condition before government is the blank.

78
00:05:15.764 --> 00:05:20.648
A, state of nature. B, social contract. C, legitimacy.

79
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.327
The answer is A: state of nature.

80
00:05:27.483 --> 00:05:29.204
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:29.504 --> 00:05:36.095
A, Authority stems from consent.. B, Authority stems of consent.. C, Authority stems by consent..

82
00:05:39.633 --> 00:05:42.968
The answer is A: Authority stems from consent..

83
00:05:44.117 --> 00:05:47.730
According to the reading, authority resting on force alone is…

84
00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:52.543
A, stable. B, rarely stable. C, legitimate.

85
00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.668
The answer is B: rarely stable.

86
00:05:59.867 --> 00:06:03.250
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:04.117 --> 00:06:07.068
What makes a government's authority legitimate, in your view?

88
00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:25.349
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:25.649 --> 00:06:53.187
In my view, a government is legitimate when its authority derives from fair procedures and the real consent of citizens. Elections, independent courts and respect for rights give people a way to hold power to account. A government that rules only by fear may be obeyed, but it is not legitimate. Legitimacy also needs to be earned continuously through fair treatment.

90
00:06:53.487 --> 00:06:55.614
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:56.517 --> 00:07:01.758
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and source-of language (derives from, stems from).

92
00:07:02.058 --> 00:07:07.578
Today's value is civic reasoning. Reason about how power and fairness should work in a society.

93
00:07:07.878 --> 00:07:10.078
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:10.378 --> 00:07:13.227
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

